n IntersectionObserver for active nav tracking, plus collapsible sections and search. I'm also mapping out the content structure for Unit 1 and Unit 2, covering definitions, tables, formulas, and the formal DFA/NFA definitions before writing the full file.\n\nWriting HTML structure...\n\nI'm working out the question distribution per sample paper, following the mark pattern from the previous year paper (1+1+2+2+4+4 marks totaling 14), and realizing \"4x6\" likely means four separate papers each with six questions. I'll draft the first paper with a theoretical focus, starting with a one-mark question about counting strings up to a given length.\n\nThen for Paper 2 I'm outlining a Mealy Machine outputting based on divisibility of 1s by 3, followed by DFA/NFA conceptual questions covering NFA power, ε-closures, language operations, DFA construction for exact 'c' counts, NFA for second-last-symbol conditions, and ε-NFA to DFA subset construction.\n\nFor Paper 3 I'm shifting focus to minimization and regular expressions, including formal DFA definitions, Σ* enumeration, regex construction for pattern-based strings, DFA avoiding \"aa\" substrings, and NFA detecting the third symbol from the right.\n\nFor Paper 4 I'm building a comprehensive mix covering Chomsky Hierarchy classification, string operations on \"baabb\", closure properties for union and intersection of specific languages, and DFA construction with combined acceptance conditions.\n\nI also need regular expression and NFA construction for substring matching, plus ε-NFA to DFA conversion with minimization, and a Moore machine tracking mod-3 remainders of binary numbers.\n\nNow I have enough exam content to assemble the full HTML file, aiming for roughly 300 lines of CSS, 600 lines of content markup, and 100 lines of JS for around 1000 lines total.\n\nI'm planning a dark-themed color palette with deep blue-black backgrounds, indigo and cyan accents, and Inter as the primary typeface, letting CSS carry the visual design while JS handles navigation.\n\nI'll write the complete HTML file to the study hub project, going section by section as one comprehensive write, aiming for content that's thorough yet not overly verbose.","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path \"C:\\Users\\tanus\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\TOC-Study-Hub\" | Out-Null; Write-Host \"Dir created\"","Cwd":"C:\\Users\\tanus","WaitMsBeforeAsync":3000,"toolAction":"Creating project directory","toolSummary":"Project directory setup"}}]}
